{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KJ9XeEvfdM69zi1tu9hBXH","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# C1 vs C5 Format Diversity Metrics (Unified)\n2\t\n3\t## 数据说明\n4\t- C1: LoRA K=4, ArmoRM top-1 curated (selected via `argmax(ArmoRM score)`)\n5\t- C5: LoRA K=4, random selection (no curation, `response[0]` as proxy)\n6\t- Population: selected responses only (2000 per generation)\n7\t- C1 scoring: ArmoRM-Llama3-8B-v0.1, batch_size=4, with single-item fallback (4 errors per 8000 pairs)\n8\t\n9\t## Results\n10\t\n11\t| Metric | C1 Gen0 | C1 Gen2 | C5 Gen0 | C5 Gen2 | C1 Δ | C5 Δ |\n12\t|--------|---------|---------|---------|---------|------|------|\n13\t| opening_unique_ratio | 0.9800 | 0.9755 | 0.9790 | 0.9750 | -0.0045 | -0.0040 |\n14\t| opening_entropy (bits) | 10.9067 | 10.9016 | 10.9057 | 10.8931 | -0.0051 | -0.0126 |\n15\t| near_duplicate_start_rate | 0.0285 | 0.0370 | 0.0300 | 0.0345 | +0.0085 | +0.0045 |\n16\t| per_response_ttr | 0.7136 | 0.7007 | 0.6997 | 0.6774 | -0.0129 | -0.0223 |\n17\t| trigram_repetition_rate | 0.0848 | 0.1007 | 0.0889 | 0.1102 | +0.0159 | +0.0213 |\n18\t\n19\t## Key Findings\n20\t\n21\t1. **Both conditions show format diversity degradation from Gen 0 to Gen 2**, confirming iterative training causes format collapse regardless of curation strategy.\n22\t\n23\t2. **C5 (random) degrades faster than C1 (curated) on 3 of 5 metrics**:\n24\t   - `per_response_ttr`: C5 drops 0.0223 vs C1 drops 0.0129 (C5 loses 74% more lexical diversity)\n25\t   - `trigram_repetition_rate`: C5 increases 0.0213 vs C1 increases 0.0159 (C5 gains 34% more repeated trigrams)\n26\t   - `opening_entropy`: C5 drops 0.0126 vs C1 drops 0.0051 (C5 loses 2.5x more opening diversity)\n27\t\n28\t3. **C1 starts with slightly higher diversity at Gen 0** (higher TTR 0.7136 vs 0.6997, lower trigram repetition 0.0848 vs 0.0889), likely because ArmoRM top-1 selection favors higher-quality, more lexically varied responses.\n29\t\n30\t4. **`near_duplicate_start_rate` is the exception**: C1 shows slightly more increase (+0.0085 vs +0.0045), suggesting ArmoRM curation may converge opening patterns even while preserving body diversity.\n31\t\n32\t5. **Overall**: ArmoRM curation (C1) provides a modest but consistent format-diversity preservation effect compared to random selection (C5). The effect is most visible in lexical diversity (TTR) and trigram repetition, less so in opening patterns.\n33\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"419c6fbb-223f-48ac-bd98-ee4399849d25","uuid":"00ad5acf-a72d-47b5-9b16-cc561514124a","timestamp":"2026-05-17T14:03:25.724Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513/projects/collapse_aware_reward_rebalancing/artifacts/format_metrics_unified.md","content":"# C1 vs C5 Format Diversity Metrics (Unified)\n\n## 数据说明\n- C1: LoRA K=4, ArmoRM top-1 curated (selected via `argmax(ArmoRM score)`)\n- C5: LoRA K=4, random selection (no curation, `response[0]` as proxy)\n- Population: selected responses only (2000 per generation)\n- C1 scoring: ArmoRM-Llama3-8B-v0.1, batch_size=4, with single-item fallback (4 errors per 8000 pairs)\n\n## Results\n\n| Metric | C1 Gen0 | C1 Gen2 | C5 Gen0 | C5 Gen2 | C1 Δ | C5 Δ |\n|--------|---------|---------|---------|---------|------|------|\n| opening_unique_ratio | 0.9800 | 0.9755 | 0.9790 | 0.9750 | -0.0045 | -0.0040 |\n| opening_entropy (bits) | 10.9067 | 10.9016 | 10.9057 | 10.8931 | -0.0051 | -0.0126 |\n| near_duplicate_start_rate | 0.0285 | 0.0370 | 0.0300 | 0.0345 | +0.0085 | +0.0045 |\n| per_response_ttr | 0.7136 | 0.7007 | 0.6997 | 0.6774 | -0.0129 | -0.0223 |\n| trigram_repetition_rate | 0.0848 | 0.1007 | 0.0889 | 0.1102 | +0.0159 | +0.0213 |\n\n## Key Findings\n\n1. **Both conditions show format diversity degradation from Gen 0 to Gen 2**, confirming iterative training causes format collapse regardless of curation strategy.\n\n2. **C5 (random) degrades faster than C1 (curated) on 3 of 5 metrics**:\n   - `per_response_ttr`: C5 drops 0.0223 vs C1 drops 0.0129 (C5 loses 74% more lexical diversity)\n   - `trigram_repetition_rate`: C5 increases 0.0213 vs C1 increases 0.0159 (C5 gains 34% more repeated trigrams)\n   - `opening_entropy`: C5 drops 0.0126 vs C1 drops 0.0051 (C5 loses 2.5x more opening diversity)\n\n3. **C1 starts with slightly higher diversity at Gen 0** (higher TTR 0.7136 vs 0.6997, lower trigram repetition 0.0848 vs 0.0889), likely because ArmoRM top-1 selection favors higher-quality, more lexically varied responses.\n\n4. **`near_duplicate_start_rate` is the exception**: C1 shows slightly more increase (+0.0085 vs +0.0045), suggesting ArmoRM curation may converge opening patterns even while preserving body diversity.\n\n5. **Overall**: ArmoRM curation (C1) provides a modest but consistent format-diversity preservation effect compared to random selection (C5). The effect is most visible in lexical diversity (TTR) and trigram repetition, less so in opening patterns.\n","numLines":33,"startLine":1,"totalLines":33}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01NVF5xx6FH118cmdEaDhYmg","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a much clearer picture. Let me note the key format metrics:\n\n**format_metrics_unified.md (selected responses, 2000 per gen):**\n- C1 (curated K=4): opening_entropy Δ = -0.0051, near_dup Δ = +0.0085, TTR Δ = -0.0129\n- C5 (random K=4): opening_entropy Δ = -0.0126, near_dup Δ = +0.0045, TTR Δ = -0.0223\n\n\n- C5 (random K=4): opening_entropy Δ = -0.7454, near_dup_start_rate Δ = +0.3662, opening_unique_ratio Δ = -0.2743\n\nThere's a significant discrepancy here—the paper claims \"-0.75 bits\" for both C1 and C5, but when I look at the full generation pool with 8000 responses, C5 shows that massive -0.7454 entropy drop. The selected subset metrics are much smaller in magnitude, suggesting the collapse signal is concentrated in the unselected responses. Looking at the K=8 curation data across the full 16000 response pool, the baseline opening entropy sits at 13.0872.\n\nNow I'm seeing the pattern: there are two different measurement contexts producing vastly different results. The selected responses show minimal changes, while the full generation pool reveals the dramatic entropy collapse the paper highlights. I have what I need to understand the discrepancy.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2904,"cache_read_input_tokens":42732,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2904},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"419c6fbb-223f-48ac-bd98-ee4399849d25","uuid":"fd40d457-315b-4332-b9f4-ed7c57e9b6ae"}
