g through the figures: Figure 2 would now display the 1.5B curated line showing clear Vendi decline alongside the 0.6B lines that remain relatively stable, creating a visual contrast that anchors the narrative. Figure 5 would introduce a scale threshold visualization to show where the model size effects become apparent.\n\nNow I'm writing the complete YAML with all relevant metrics included for each experiment, being careful to distinguish between the full trajectory data and the key comparison points like gen2 for the 1.5B runs versus the 3-gen structure of the 0.6B experiments.\n\nI'm also tracking the format entropy carefully—the full generation pool shows dramatic collapse (opening_entropy dropping from ~12.8 bits to ~10.95 bits, a -1.85 bit loss for 1.5B worst case), which is much more severe than what appears in the selected 2000-response subset where changes are only -0.0051 to -0.0126 bits. For the paper, I need to use the full-pool numbers since they reveal the actual format collapse happening across all K candidates.\n\nNow I'm ready to structure the YAML output—each experiment needs its expected_results as a flat dict of metrics, using actual observed values for completed runs and worst-case projections for queued ones.\n\nLet me verify the mapping first: C2 from the paper (LoRA K=8 curated) corresponds to plan_002, and the entropy drop of -1.13 bits matches what I'm seeing in the collapse data. Good, that checks out.\n\nWriting the YAML structure now... I'm reframing the narrative away from universal resilience and instead positioning this as a scale-conditional finding: semantic resilience holds up to 0.6B but collapses at 1.5B, marking a critical threshold where curation breaks down. The 0.6B evidence is solid and becomes our \"small-model safety zone,\" while the 1.5B curated collapse serves as a cautionary signal for practitioners scaling up. The missing 1.5B random baseline from the infrastructure issue is the biggest vulnerability for reviewers to 